[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C behaviour.** `stb_perlin_noise3_wrap_nonpow2` computes `px % x_wrap2`. With
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `px == INT_MIN` and `x_wrap2 == -1` the quotient is not representable, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `idiv` gcc emits at `-O0` raises `#DE`. The process is *killed by* `SIGFPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m having printed nothing: empty stdout, empty stderr, `WIFSIGNALED` with signal 8.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **What the translation did.** It detected the case correctly but reported it with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `std::process::exit(136)`. A shell prints `136` for both outcomes, which is why
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m this hid for a while, but they are different wait statuses: `waitpid` reports
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `WIFEXITED`/code 136 for the exit and `WIFSIGNALED`/signal 8 for the C program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Any harness that inspects the exit *status* rather than a shell's rendering of it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sees the difference:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ printf '5 nan 0.5 0.5 -1 0 0 0 0 0 0 0' | ./c_src/build/driver ; # returncode -8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ printf '5 nan 0.5 0.5 -1 0 0 0 0 0 0 0' | ./translation/.../driver ; # returncode 136
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/csig.rs`: reset the signal disposition to `SIG_DFL` and `raise` the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signal, so the Rust process dies the same way. Resetting first is necessary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because the Rust runtime installs its own `SIGSEGV`/`SIGBUS` handler for stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overflow reporting, and a handled signal would not terminate the process.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. Table reads past the last mapped page printed a number instead of dying of `SIGSEGV`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs.** `which == 5` with a wrap larger than the tables, which makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x0 = px % x_wrap2` exceed the 512-entry `stb__perlin_randtab`. A wrap of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2 000 000 000 makes the index equal to `floor(x)`, so these select one exact
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m offset each:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5 4030.5 0.5 0.5 2000000000 0 0 0 0 0 0 0     -> C prints -0.25
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5 4031.5 0.5 0.5 2000000000 0 0 0 0 0 0 0     -> C dies of SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5 0.5 0.5 100000.5 0 0 65536 0 0 0 0 0        -> C dies of SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C behaviour.** The reads are out of bounds — undefined behaviour — but in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compiled program they are ordinary loads at fixed addresses, and the non-PIE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m image places the objects deterministically:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x400000  first mapped page of the program image
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x405020  .data begins: 32 bytes of zero padding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x405040  stb__perlin_randtab           512 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x405240  stb__perlin_randtab_grad_idx  512 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x405440  basis.0                       192 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x405500  _edata; .bss and the rest of the page read as zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x406000  first unmapped page
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So an index into `randtab` reads real table data, then the gradient-index table,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then `basis`, then zeros — and past `0x406000` the process dies of `SIGSEGV`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both edges were measured against the C program: index 4030 is the last one that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m works and 4031 is the first that faults (4031 rather than 4032 because the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m function also reads `randtab[x0 + 1]`), and on the low side −20544 works while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m −20545 faults, matching `0x405040 ± ` the mapping bounds exactly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **What the translation did.** It modelled the three tables and the padding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m faithfully but treated *everything* outside that 1 248-byte window as zero, so it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printed a value where the C program crashed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/stb_perlin.rs`: the image model now carries the bounds of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mapped address range (`OFF_MAP_LO`/`OFF_MAP_HI`, i.e. `[0x400000, 0x406000)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expressed relative to `&stb__perlin_randtab`). `data_byte` raises `SIGSEGV`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m outside them, and still returns zero for the mapped-but-empty region above
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `_edata`. This turned 497 of the 528 signal deaths in the out-of-bounds corpus
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m into exact matches; the remainder are covered by the known limitation below.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. Index arithmetic panicked in a debug build where the C wraps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs.** The same `which == 5` out-of-range wraps as above. Only visible in a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m debug build, which is what `cargo test` uses — the release profile wrapped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m silently, so this class passed under `cargo build --release` and would have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failed under `cargo test`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C behaviour.** `stb__perlin_randtab[r0 + y0]` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stb__perlin_randtab_grad_idx[r00 + z0]` add an `int` index that can be near
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` to a table byte. gcc at `-O0` wraps.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **What the translation did.** Used plain `+` on `i32`, which panics with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "attempt to add with overflow" when debug assertions are on.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/stb_perlin.rs`: all index arithmetic and the fractal octave counter
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m now use `wrapping_add`, so the behaviour no longer depends on the build profile.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The whole corpus is checked against both `target/release/driver` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `target/debug/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known limitation: reads *below* `.data` cannot be reproduced, and the C program does not reproduce them either
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs.** `which == 5` with a *negative* wrap and a coordinate that makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `px` negative. `x0 = px % x_wrap2` is then negative, and the `if (x0 < 0) x0 +=
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x_wrap2` fixup makes it *more* negative rather than less, leaving `x0` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `(2*wrap, wrap]`. Once the index is below −32 it reaches past the zero padding at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the start of `.data` into `.got.plt`, `.got`, `.dynamic` and the read-only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m segment before them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Those bytes are produced by the linker and the dynamic loader, not by the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m source, so no translation of `stb_perlin.h` can predict them. More to the point,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **the C program is not deterministic for these inputs**: `.got.plt` holds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addresses inside `ld.so`, which ASLR relocates on every run. Eight consecutive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs of one input give eight different answers:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ for i in $(seq 8); do printf '5 -1746.157 1415.769 1959.224 -838 209 -928 210 0 0 0 0' \
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       | ./c_src/build/driver; done
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m -0.269727886
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0.0150961876
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m -0.133434951
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m -0.0406534076
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0.118725419
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0.368001729
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0.193987966
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m -0.0793716908
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 15 of 400 randomly generated negative-wrap inputs behave this way. There is no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical output to match, so these inputs are deliberately excluded from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the test corpus. Everything the C source *does* determine is modelled: indices
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from −32 (the start of `.data`) up to 4031 (the end of the mapped page) are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduced exactly, which `tests/differential.rs` checks offset by offset.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The negative-wrap cases that stay inside the zero padding — wraps of −1 through
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m −16, where `x0` never drops below −32 — are included and do match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Verified with no mismatch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were audited against the C and confirmed already correct; the tests now pin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m them so they stay that way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` reads across newlines.** `%d`/`%f` skip leading whitespace, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   12 conversions happily span lines, blank lines, `\v`, `\f` and `\r`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf`'s return value is ignored.** Any conversion that fails stops the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   scan and leaves that variable and all later ones at their `0`/`0.0f`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   initialisers, so `printf` still runs. Every prefix length of the 12 tokens is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   covered, as is a matching failure in each of the 12 slots.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **glibc `%f` quirks.** An incomplete exponent is consumed but contributes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   nothing (`1e` and `1e-` convert to 1); `inf` and `infinity` are accepted but
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   any partial prefix (`in`, `infi`, `infinit`) is a matching failure; `nan` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   accepted while the `nan(n-char-sequence)` form is not, so `nan(x)` leaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `(x)` unread; hex floats (`0x1.8p+1`, `0x.8p1`) are accepted while a bare `0x`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is a matching failure.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%d` overflow.** glibc saturates to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` and the result is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   then truncated to `int`, so `99999999999999999999` becomes −1 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2147483648` becomes `INT_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`(int)` conversion of out-of-range floats.** `cvttss2si` yields `INT_MIN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   for NaN and for anything that does not fit, in both directions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **NaN sign.** `addss`/`subss`/`mulss` return the *destination* operand when it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is a NaN, which decides whether `printf` writes `nan` or `-nan`. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation mirrors the operand order gcc emits at `-O0` (`src/sse.rs`), and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   all 10 368 combinations of specials across the six `which` arms agree.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printf("%.9g")`.** `%e` style when the decimal exponent is `< -4` or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `>= 9`, `%f` style otherwise, trailing zeros stripped, no trailing point, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-0`, `nan`, `-nan`, `inf`, `-inf` spelled the way glibc spells them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Truncation of `seed` and of the octave counter** to `unsigned char`, so both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   fold every 256 and negative values fold the same way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`octaves <= 0`** never enters the fractal loop, so the result is `0.0f`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **The two constant tables and `basis`** were compared entry by entry against
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `stb_perlin.h`: both 512-entry tables consist of two identical 256-entry
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   halves, matching the base tables in `src/stb_perlin.rs`, and all 12 `basis`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rows match with the unwritten fourth column zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage of the C source
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The enumerated inputs were replayed through a gcov-instrumented build of the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (configured out-of-tree so `c_src/` is untouched):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/main.c        lines 100.00% of 33   branches 100.00% of 7   (all taken)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/stb_perlin.h  lines 100.00% of 121  branches 100.00% of 20  (all taken)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every line and both directions of every branch in the C program are exercised by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the corpus.
